# deletedIds Entry-Point Code Reading Notes

The entry point is in `static/jquery/jquery.js`; start reading around line 45. This appears first:

```js
var deletedIds = [];
var document = window.document;
var slice = deletedIds.slice;
var concat = deletedIds.concat;
```

The local declarations in this section start with `deletedIds`, initialized to an empty array; `document` is taken from `window.document` immediately after that. When reading later references, return to these four lines first to confirm that the names come from local variables. In particular, don't confuse the name `document` with the page object in the file path.

The same area also declares `push`, `indexOf`, and `class2type`. The first two names should be recorded together with `slice` and `concat` next to the array entry point; although `class2type` is nearby, its name differs from those of this group of array methods, so mark it separately first.

This tracing pass starts with the initialization of `deletedIds`, then looks at the aliases for `slice` and `concat`, and finally works backward from the references to `push` and `indexOf` to find when the array is written to. Where array members are added, whether the array is cleared anywhere, and whether `class2type` is involved in this path are still unconfirmed.
